# Assignment 1: Linux File Permissions and Windows Registry Monitoring

## Overview

This individual security assignment explored access control, file-system permissions, and security monitoring across Linux and Windows environments. The work included configuring and testing Linux user permissions in a Fedora virtual machine, evaluating physical-access risks using a live operating system, and analyzing Windows Registry activity using Process Monitor.

## Topics Covered

- Linux users, groups, and file-system permissions
- Access-control validation and permission testing
- Fedora Linux virtual-machine configuration
- Risks of physical access and bootable live media
- File encryption and confidentiality considerations
- Windows Registry attack scenarios
- Process Monitor filtering for registry-event analysis

## Tools Used

- Fedora Security Lab
- VirtualBox or VMware
- Windows
- Sysinternals Process Monitor

## Skills Demonstrated

- Linux administration and permission management
- Security testing and access-control analysis
- Virtual-machine setup and snapshots
- Windows endpoint monitoring
- Registry-event filtering and investigation
- Technical security documentation

